1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,754
The training cluster is air-gapped. It has
no network route to your Git server, and

2
00:00:09,794 --> 00:00:15,047
it needs your code every week. Your CTO
asks: how do we ship it, and how do we ship

3
00:00:15,087 --> 00:00:16,132
only what is new?

4
00:00:16,472 --> 00:00:21,794
The answer is a file. One full bundle to
start, then an incremental bundle per week.

5
00:00:21,834 --> 00:00:27,156
And the second half of the question hides
the risk: an incremental bundle names what

6
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:32,326
the other side must already have. Lose one
shipment, and the next one is refused.

7
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:35,326
Keep that lost shipment
in mind. It comes back.

8
00:00:37,166 --> 00:00:40,913
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:46,003
You created and cloned bundles earlier in
the course, in the remote operations and

10
00:00:46,043 --> 00:00:51,155
recovery chapters. Two things remain. The
incremental bundle with its prerequisite,

11
00:00:51,195 --> 00:00:56,121
and --bundle-uri, which seeds a clone from
a bundle and takes only the remainder

12
00:00:56,161 --> 00:00:57,114
from the server.

13
00:00:57,454 --> 00:01:02,898
Then we close the scale topic with one
table from section 26.15: which feature

14
00:01:02,938 --> 00:01:08,734
matters when. Its message is that most
repositories need none of what the last four

15
00:01:08,774 --> 00:01:09,718
videos showed.

16
00:01:10,058 --> 00:01:16,204
Replays: labs/ch26/bundles.sh, and the
replay of Lab 18.3 up to its checkpoint.

17
00:01:16,244 --> 00:01:22,546
git bundle create is 🟢 SAFE: it writes a
file. git bundle verify changes nothing.

18
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:25,798
After this video you can:

19
00:01:26,138 --> 00:01:29,537
Create a full and an incremental
bundle and read a bundle's header.

20
00:01:29,877 --> 00:01:33,973
Explain what a prerequisite is and what
happens when one shipment is lost.

21
00:01:34,313 --> 00:01:36,986
Clone from a bundle and
continue from the network.

22
00:01:37,327 --> 00:01:41,645
Say for a repository of a given size
which scale features are worth enabling.

23
00:01:43,485 --> 00:01:48,370
In one sentence: a bundle is a file that
holds a pack and the refs that go with it,

24
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:53,295
and an incremental bundle is one that names
commits the receiver must already have.

25
00:01:53,335 --> 00:01:57,626
A pack is one file of many objects,
and a ref is a name such as a branch.

26
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:02,971
Why does it exist? Because a fetch needs
a conversation, and some sites can't have

27
00:02:03,011 --> 00:02:06,971
one. A bundle is the server's half
of a fetch, written to a file.

28
00:02:07,311 --> 00:02:12,027
How do you make an incremental one?
From a range: git bundle create <file>

29
00:02:12,067 --> 00:02:17,427
<old>..<new>. A range is the commits reachable
from the new end and not from the old

30
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:22,569
one. Such a bundle contains only the
objects that the range adds, and it records

31
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:27,904
<old> as a prerequisite. git bundle verify,
and every fetch from the bundle, refuse

32
00:02:27,944 --> 00:02:32,854
a repository that lacks the prerequisite.
On screen is today's range: the two

33
00:02:32,894 --> 00:02:34,268
commits after 100bb99.

34
00:02:34,608 --> 00:02:38,458
Internally, the file is a short
text header followed by a pack.

35
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:42,904
A signature line. One line per
prerequisite, starting with a minus sign.

36
00:02:42,944 --> 00:02:47,843
One line per ref. An empty line. Then the
pack. An incremental bundle is thin on

37
00:02:47,883 --> 00:02:52,845
purpose, and its deltas, objects stored
as differences from other objects, may be

38
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:57,414
based on objects it doesn't contain.
That's why the prerequisite is a hard

39
00:02:57,454 --> 00:02:59,328
requirement and not a courtesy.

40
00:02:59,668 --> 00:03:05,528
The second feature: git clone --bundle-uri=<uri>
takes the bulk of a clone from a

41
00:03:05,568 --> 00:03:10,699
bundle, for example from a CDN, and
only the remainder from the server.

42
00:03:10,739 --> 00:03:16,234
Servers can advertise bundle URIs
themselves, and clients ignore that unless

43
00:03:16,274 --> 00:03:22,352
transfer.bundleURI is set. The option needs
Git 2.38 or later, and it's incompatible

44
00:03:22,392 --> 00:03:23,299
with --depth.

45
00:03:23,639 --> 00:03:27,419
When should you not use a bundle?
As a backup of everything.

46
00:03:27,459 --> 00:03:30,730
A bundle carries no reflogs,
hooks or configuration.

47
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:34,139
Failure mode and recovery,
from the chapter's table.

48
00:03:34,179 --> 00:03:37,666
The symptom is Repository lacks
these prerequisite commits.

49
00:03:37,706 --> 00:03:41,791
The diagnosis: an incremental bundle
arrived without its predecessor.

50
00:03:41,831 --> 00:03:46,275
The fix: apply the missing bundle, or
cut a new one from what the site has.

51
00:03:46,315 --> 00:03:50,220
The prevention: number the bundles,
and record the basis in a tag.

52
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,582
Think of weekly instalments
of a serial sent by post.

53
00:03:55,622 --> 00:04:01,226
The first parcel is the whole story so far.
Each later parcel begins "continued from

54
00:04:01,266 --> 00:04:06,870
page 412". If you hold page 412, you can
read on. If a parcel was lost, the next one

55
00:04:06,910 --> 00:04:11,977
is useless to you until the gap is
filled, and it says so on its first page.

56
00:04:12,317 --> 00:04:17,194
The model breaks in one respect: the sender
doesn't have to resend the lost parcel.

57
00:04:17,234 --> 00:04:22,170
Because the sender has the whole history,
a new parcel can be cut from any point the

58
00:04:22,210 --> 00:04:27,086
receiver still has. The prerequisite is a
statement about the receiver's state, not

59
00:04:27,126 --> 00:04:28,745
about the sequence of files.

60
00:04:30,585 --> 00:04:36,110
On the left, the connected site. Its
main has moved two commits past 100bb99.

61
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:42,109
On the right, the isolated site, which was
cloned from the full bundle when 100bb99

62
00:04:42,149 --> 00:04:42,976
was the tip.

63
00:04:43,316 --> 00:04:48,361
The file in the middle: a header and a
pack. The line with the minus sign is the

64
00:04:48,401 --> 00:04:53,509
prerequisite. Find 100bb99 on both sides.
The bundle applies on the right because

65
00:04:53,549 --> 00:04:58,656
that commit is there. Carried into an empty
repository, the same file is refused.

66
00:05:02,996 --> 00:05:07,889
The second bundle is made from a range.
What will verify say that it didn't say

67
00:05:07,929 --> 00:05:09,575
about the first? I'll wait.

68
00:05:14,415 --> 00:05:18,345
For the full bundle: "The bundle
records a complete history."

69
00:05:18,385 --> 00:05:23,748
For the update: two refs, and "The bundle
requires this ref", followed by the ID of

70
00:05:23,788 --> 00:05:24,725
commit 100bb99.

71
00:05:25,065 --> 00:05:28,425
The header as text. The
signature line, # v2 git bundle.

72
00:05:28,465 --> 00:05:32,857
The prerequisite line, starting with
a minus sign. Then one line per ref.

73
00:05:32,897 --> 00:05:34,921
The full bundle has no minus line.

74
00:05:35,261 --> 00:05:38,526
A quick quiz. site-b was
cloned from the full bundle.

75
00:05:38,566 --> 00:05:43,640
empty is a new repository. Which of them
accepts the update, and what exactly does

76
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:45,698
the other one print? Your answer?

77
00:05:53,538 --> 00:05:58,671
The site that has 100bb99 verifies and
fetches the update, and main fast-forwards.

78
00:05:58,711 --> 00:06:03,782
The empty repository is refused twice, by
verify and by clone, each time with the

79
00:06:03,822 --> 00:06:07,567
name of the missing commit.
That's the hook's lost shipment.

80
00:06:07,907 --> 00:06:12,475
On the graph of site-b, the fetch
moves origin/main two commits on.

81
00:06:12,515 --> 00:06:15,707
Then the fast-forward
moves main up to 6d3aa59.

82
00:06:16,047 --> 00:06:19,814
In the last video a plain clone
sent eight wants and no haves.

83
00:06:19,854 --> 00:06:23,868
What does a clone seeded from a
bundle send? Make your prediction.

84
00:06:28,708 --> 00:06:32,919
Five have lines and three want lines. The
seeded clone first unpacked the bundle,

85
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:37,117
kept its refs in a namespace of their own,
and then told the server what it had.

86
00:06:37,157 --> 00:06:41,316
One detail the textbook records: on this
machine the namespace is refs/bundles/,

87
00:06:41,356 --> 00:06:43,940
while the manual of git
clone writes refs/bundle/.

88
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:51,262
The lab adds the bookkeeping. After
the full bundle is cut, a tag,

89
00:06:51,302 --> 00:06:55,030
lastbundle/site-b, records
what the other site now has.

90
00:06:55,070 --> 00:06:58,113
The manual of git bundle
uses a tag for this.

91
00:06:58,453 --> 00:07:04,343
The update is cut from lastbundle/site-b..main,
and then the tag is moved forward.

92
00:07:04,383 --> 00:07:10,417
Moving a tag with -f is a deliberate exception
here: this tag is a private bookmark,

93
00:07:10,457 --> 00:07:11,429
not a release.

94
00:07:11,769 --> 00:07:17,023
And work travels back the same way: the
isolated site bundles origin/main..main, and

95
00:07:17,063 --> 00:07:21,749
the connected site fetches it into a
remote-tracking ref and fast-forwards.

96
00:07:21,789 --> 00:07:25,971
The lab's failure scenario loses
one update. The recovery is yours.

97
00:07:26,311 --> 00:07:30,870
Now the closing table for the whole scale
topic. Automatic maintenance: always.

98
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:35,585
Leave it on. Scheduled maintenance with
prefetch: large repositories on developer

99
00:07:35,625 --> 00:07:40,417
machines. The commit-graph with changed-path
filters: long history and path-limited

100
00:07:40,457 --> 00:07:45,016
queries. Geometric repack, multi-pack-index
and bitmaps: servers and very large

101
00:07:45,056 --> 00:07:49,731
clones. The file-system monitor and the
untracked cache: hundreds of thousands of

102
00:07:49,771 --> 00:07:50,546
tracked files.

103
00:07:50,886 --> 00:07:55,583
Sparse-checkout and the sparse index: a wide
tree of which each person needs little.

104
00:07:55,623 --> 00:07:59,361
That's the next video. Partial
clone: much blob content in history.

105
00:07:59,401 --> 00:08:03,590
Shallow clone: throwaway builds of one
snapshot. And checking what the pack

106
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:07,030
contains: a repository much
larger than its content explains.

107
00:08:07,370 --> 00:08:11,426
The figures the textbook cites for
these rows are each reporter's own.

108
00:08:11,466 --> 00:08:12,831
One deserves a sentence.

109
00:08:13,171 --> 00:08:18,062
Dropbox reported in 2026 that its monorepo
shrank from 87 GB to 20 GB through

110
00:08:18,102 --> 00:08:23,249
repacking, not through deleting history.
The cause was the heuristic by which Git

111
00:08:23,289 --> 00:08:28,308
chooses delta candidates, which paired
translation files of different languages

112
00:08:28,348 --> 00:08:33,495
because of the directory layout. The fix
was a repack on the server with a larger

113
00:08:33,535 --> 00:08:38,683
window and depth. You learned in the
packfiles video that a delta is "between two

114
00:08:38,723 --> 00:08:43,037
objects chosen for similarity". Here
the choice went wrong at scale.

115
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:46,525
Five mistakes to watch for.

116
00:08:46,865 --> 00:08:50,734
Sending an incremental bundle to a
site that missed the previous one.

117
00:08:50,774 --> 00:08:55,378
Root cause: the bundle is thin and records
a prerequisite commit that the receiver

118
00:08:55,418 --> 00:08:56,398
must already have.

119
00:08:56,738 --> 00:09:01,332
Cutting each update "since last week" from
memory. Root cause: the basis must be

120
00:09:01,372 --> 00:09:04,402
what the other site actually
has; record it in a tag.

121
00:09:04,742 --> 00:09:09,975
Treating a bundle as a full backup. Root
cause: a bundle carries no reflogs, hooks

122
00:09:10,015 --> 00:09:11,068
or configuration.

123
00:09:11,408 --> 00:09:14,491
Tuning a small repository
with every scale feature.

124
00:09:14,531 --> 00:09:19,635
Root cause: each feature is configuration
the next person must understand; automatic

125
00:09:19,675 --> 00:09:23,065
maintenance is enough until
a measured counter is large.

126
00:09:23,405 --> 00:09:29,138
Combining --bundle-uri with --depth. Root
cause: the two options are incompatible;

127
00:09:29,178 --> 00:09:32,517
not every combination of
transfer options works.

128
00:09:34,357 --> 00:09:38,349
Now, out of the lab. An ML team
trains on an air-gapped cluster.

129
00:09:38,389 --> 00:09:43,264
Every Friday a release engineer cuts
orbit-update-<n>.bundle from the tag that

130
00:09:43,304 --> 00:09:48,556
records the last shipment to main, moves
the tag, and hands the numbered file to the

131
00:09:48,596 --> 00:09:53,786
operator of the cluster. On the cluster,
the operator runs git bundle verify before

132
00:09:53,826 --> 00:09:54,353
fetching.

133
00:09:54,693 --> 00:09:59,944
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. One
week a file isn't delivered, and the next

134
00:09:59,984 --> 00:10:04,471
one arrives. Write down what git
bundle verify prints, and one way out.

135
00:10:04,511 --> 00:10:05,108
I'll wait.

136
00:10:09,948 --> 00:10:14,600
One week a file isn't delivered. The
following Friday, verify prints Repository

137
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:17,688
lacks these prerequisite
commits with one commit ID.

138
00:10:17,728 --> 00:10:22,677
Nothing is damaged on either side. The
engineer has two options: deliver the missing

139
00:10:22,717 --> 00:10:27,547
bundle first, or cut a new bundle from the
last commit the cluster reports having.

140
00:10:27,587 --> 00:10:32,476
The team's prevention is already in place:
numbered bundles, and the basis recorded

141
00:10:32,516 --> 00:10:33,010
in a tag.

142
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:40,691
Your turn. Do Lab 18.3, "A bundle round
trip", in lab-manual/m18-transfer-scale.md.

143
00:10:40,731 --> 00:10:46,358
Before each git bundle verify, predict
whether it will say "complete history" or

144
00:10:46,398 --> 00:10:52,168
name a required commit, and which commit.
Before the failure scenario, predict the

145
00:10:52,208 --> 00:10:53,159
exact refusal.

146
00:10:53,499 --> 00:11:00,128
The challenge is Exercise 18.9, "The
clone that believes it is up to date", in

147
00:11:00,168 --> 00:11:02,778
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

148
00:11:04,618 --> 00:11:07,617
Question 450 of the CTO question bank:

149
00:11:07,957 --> 00:11:13,070
A strong answer describes the first shipment
and the weekly one separately, and says

150
00:11:13,110 --> 00:11:17,856
how the sender knows what the receiver
has. For the lost shipment it gives the

151
00:11:17,896 --> 00:11:22,458
symptom in Git's words, explains why
Git refuses, and offers both ways out.

152
00:11:22,498 --> 00:11:24,912
It mentions what a bundle doesn't carry.

153
00:11:26,752 --> 00:11:31,822
Let's land this. A file carries history
across a gap, if the other side holds the

154
00:11:31,862 --> 00:11:33,210
commit it starts from.

155
00:11:33,550 --> 00:11:35,210
You should now be able to say:

156
00:11:35,550 --> 00:11:38,958
A bundle is a header of refs and
prerequisites followed by a pack.

157
00:11:39,298 --> 00:11:44,183
An incremental bundle is cut from a range
and applies only where its prerequisite

158
00:11:44,223 --> 00:11:45,034
commit exists.

159
00:11:45,374 --> 00:11:50,464
--bundle-uri seeds a clone from a bundle;
the clone then offers what the bundle

160
00:11:50,504 --> 00:11:51,568
brought as haves.

161
00:11:51,908 --> 00:11:55,209
Record what the other site has in
a tag, and number the bundles.

162
00:11:55,549 --> 00:11:59,923
Measure the dimension that is large
before choosing a scale feature; most

163
00:11:59,963 --> 00:12:02,644
repositories need only
automatic maintenance.

164
00:12:04,484 --> 00:12:13,165
Read sections 26.14 to 26.18 of Chapter
26 and do the Practice section 26.20.

165
00:12:13,504 --> 00:12:18,008
Today you shipped history without a
network, and you closed the scale topic.

166
00:12:18,048 --> 00:12:22,911
Do the lab before the next video: monorepo
versus polyrepo, and sparse-checkout in

167
00:12:22,951 --> 00:12:26,677
cone mode. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

168
00:12:26,717 --> 00:12:28,112
See you in the next one.
